WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.540
I like to think that as the world's first
robot lawyer,

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.740
DoNotPay will have succeeded  if the word 'lawyer'

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.580
is completely removed from the dictionary
for average people.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.680
Joshua Browder is trying to upend the legal
services industry.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.680
His company, DoNotPay, has built an AI-powered
chatbot

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.040
that interviews users about their legal problems
in plain English,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.860
then uses their answers to complete and submit
legal paperwork on their behalf.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.400
The 21-year-old entrepreneur from the UK,
who taught himself to code with YouTube videos,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.060
lives and works with his 8-person team out
of the same Palo Alto house

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.780
that Mark Zuckerberg rented during his first
summer in California building Facebook.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.520
DoNotPay initially focused on fighting parking
tickets,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.120
and Browder says it’s succeeded about half
the time,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.000
saving users $16 million in fines over its
first 3 years.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.620
Now, operating on just over $1 million in
venture capital funding,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.280
the company is extending its legal services
to a broad range of issues—all free of charge.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.360
There are such fundamental issues where the
small bit of software can help millions of people.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.260
People don't even know which court house to
go to, or what documents to file

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.840
and if you can just help with that, then you
can solve maybe half the problem.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.740
You started with parking tickets, because
it's a sort of regressive tax on a lot of people

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.260
that makes the poorest among us pay the most.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.740
And in a lot of these cases people don't even
know that they do have some recourse, right?

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.660
So how do you educate people and how can they
find out how you can help them.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.451
So, for example, for parking tickets it actually
plays a game of 20 questions with you

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.280
to figure out a legally sound defense.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.940
It will as you 'was the parking bay too small?'

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.700
or 'were the signs hard to read?'

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.439
So we're not expecting people to come in knowing
what they can do

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:41.510
just if they have any issue that bothers them,
come to DoNotPay

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.930
and we'll get you your money back.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.460
DoNotPay’s app even monitors users’ accounts

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.680
and can automatically rebook plane tickets
when prices drop,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.880
request refunds when banks charge illegal
overdraft fees,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.259
and help users reclaim security deposits from
shady landlords.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.000
The flagship feature is, if you have a problem
with anyone,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.580
be it a corporation or your neighbor, you
can sue them by pressing a button.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.700
What happens when people actually get to court
to contest these things?

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.100
So at that point we actually give you a script
which you can read yourself.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.360
In the future I think robots will actually
be allowed in court.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.400
But until that day comes, people actually
have to show up themselves.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.880
But they can almost be a conduit for the robot.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.180
What we give you is very legally sound

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.940
and you can actually play a game of almost
learning what you have to say

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.940
with the robot lawyer in the app.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:28.560
Browder says the legal industry is so ripe
for disruption through software

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.620
because most services involve nothing more
than standardized processes and boilerplate language.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.440
I'm very lucky, although I hate lawyers, we
actually have a few good ones who are helping us.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.880
50 states with a bunch of different jurisdictions
within them

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.900
you cover that entire gamut of different legal
structures?

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.640
Yeah, at the end of the day lawyers like to
make it seem like the law is rocket science

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.840
but in reality, the law is society's operating
system

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.580
and the rules should just be able to be coded
without some fancy AI.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.900
We have got a bit of it on top but really
it's just a rules based system.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.420
Given that, why are legal services so expensive?

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.320
Right now, if you're a lawyer, there's no
incentive to innovate.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.300
Instead you can just be making millions of
dollars a year

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.760
by charging clients huge amounts of money.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.160
And so because lawyers control the legal system

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.800
and no one else really understands it, they've
kept it that way.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.120
And I think it will take people outside of
the legal profession like software

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.980
to force them to change.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.040
And what's your response to people that say,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.900
you know, you're delving into things like
immigration,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.020
this is not just a standardized parking ticket,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.870
you only have a few lawyers helping you,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.840
you can't possibly be providing the level
of service that a trained legal professional would.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.000
I think we really have a long way to go, but
on the other hand,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.500
if it really is that different for every person,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.920
doesn't that mean it's unfair?

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.420
Do you also hope to sort of incentivize the
broader legal system

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.850
to become more simple and fair in this way?

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:51.140
Yes, and I think the only way to do that is
by doing a bottom up approach.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.400
I like to think of it as DDoSing the legal
system to make it better.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.320
One of the most exciting things we have coming
out

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.440
is something that can get you a DMV appointment
in a few days rather than months.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.960
By phoning up the DMV hundreds of times a
day and talking to their automated scheduling system

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.880
to try and look for a cancellation for you.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.880
So this sounds great on my end, sounds like
a nightmare from a bureaucrat's end,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.300
or regulator or someone who works at the DMV.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.220
Have you gotten any push back on any of these
initiatives?

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.680
We think hard about doing it in an ethical
way

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.200
so we're only talking to their robots, not
real human beings, holding up the phone lines.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.880
And in fact a lot of governments welcome DoNotPay.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.860
When DoNotPay Parking Tickets came to Los
Angeles

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.200
they said 'we love it' because people write
such gibberish in their appeals

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.340
that at least DoNotPay's appeals are standardized
and well written.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.000
So I think that in the long run, the governments
will build their own systems

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.720
for dealing with all of this traffic

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.200
which will lead to a much fairer outcome for
everyone.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.800
DoNotPay doesn’t charge for any of its legal
help.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.980
This way the company can serve clients who
can’t afford to hire lawyers,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.620
such as refugees seeking asylum and homeless
people applying for public housing in the UK.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.620
The company is also working on a service that
helps navigate the US visa process.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:05.100
All of these processes are so bureaucratic
that if you have no resources at all

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.800
it really is impossible to get the help you
need.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.580
So DoNotPay is a completely free public service.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.240
So how do you sustain yourself, how are you
making money?

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.920
Although we have to make money to sustain ourselves,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.120
we're not going to be doing it by charging
for our legal services.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.720
Maybe one day we will help so many people
for example fly airlines

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.660
that we will learn how to do an airline ourselves,
maybe DoNotPay airlines.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:30.380
That's a big example but we plan to replace
the people we're fighting,

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.600
that's how we'll make money.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.920
But I'm not focused on that for the moment
because everything's fully automated.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.880
We're very cost efficient.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.000
And there are other similar services

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.180
or at least ones that allow you to automatically
contest parking tickets

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.200
but they make money by taking the slice of
the revenue that they return to people.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.080
Why didn't you adopt that sort of model?

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.760
50% of injustice is still injustice

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.460
and by profiting off the problems, these companies
actually have an incentive to perpetuate them.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.220
Which is why they haven't been solved.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.480
I think the biggest example are all these
tax filing services,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.820
which are now lobbying the government to keep
tax filing complicated.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:10.120
The government has tried to have a much simpler
tax filing process

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.301
but the companies, publicly listed companies
that make money off that sort of thing

00:06:14.301 --> 00:06:17.180
are saying 'no we want it to stay complicated.'

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.140
So DoNotPay doesn't have any of the conflict
of interest.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.300
But Browder does advocate for more consumer
protection laws.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.350
DoNotPay never relies on corporate policies

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.260
to get customers money back on things like
airline tickets,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.180
using only regulations buried in fine print.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.140
The way we do it is through these obscure
Department of Transportation rules

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.160
that allow the most non refundable ticket
to become refundable.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.340
And that's really important because laws are
hard to change.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.480
Of course all these corporations have lobbyists

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.680
but it's much harder for them to repeal airline
protections

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.220
than it is for them to just change a customer
service policy overnight.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.520
Do you feel that these consumer protections
are more necessary in the airline industry

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.690
or because, like you were saying earlier,

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.680
a lot of what you do is help people navigate
complexity

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.980
complexity usually ends up hurting the less
well off

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.470
because they're not able to fund actually
going through it, don't have the time.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:10.320
So in other areas, where, like tax preparation,
it's better if it's more simplistic.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.300
Why is the regulatory push in the airline
space a problem?

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:18.040
I'm from the UK and one of the biggest differences
I've noticed between the US and the UK

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.220
is that in the US it's the land of the corporation.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.780
In the UK there are so many rights that everyone
has, for example,

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.070
if the airline changes your flight time, you
have a right to cancel.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.800
I think that's a very reasonable right for
people to have.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.200
And DoNotPay helps people exercise those rights.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.660
So it's not about complexity, it's just about
giving people what they deserve

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.020
and what's just and fair.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:42.500
So in the long run, how do you see DoNotPay
sort of expanding

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.889
what space do you see it occupying and what
do you think the future is of legal services

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.090
once everything can be automated, including
a robot lawyer coming with you to a courtroom.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.980
In silicon valley companies like AirBnB and
Twitter

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:56.500
have succeeded by adding words to the dictionary.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.820
You can tweet something or snap something.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.480
I think DoNotPay will have succeeded

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.440
if the word 'lawyer' is completely removed
from the dictionary for average people.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.860
There's a 200 billion dollar legal industry
at the moment

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.940
and DoNotPay will hopefully one day make it
free for everyone.

